DORA requires financial entities, other than microenterprises, to establish, maintain, and review a sound and comprehensive digital operational resilience testing programme as part of their ICT risk-management framework, to assess preparedness for ICT-related incidents, identify weaknesses and gaps, and promptly implement corrective measures [1]. The programme must include a range of assessments, tests, methodologies, practices, and tools applied in accordance with Articles 25 and 26 [1].

The testing must follow a risk-based approach, taking into account the evolving ICT risk landscape, specific risks the entity faces, the criticality of information assets and services, and other appropriate factors [1].

The available documents also say that:
- tests must be undertaken by independent parties, internal or external; if internal, conflicts of interest must be avoided and sufficient resources dedicated [3];
- issues revealed by tests must be prioritised, classified, remedied, and validated to ensure weaknesses are fully addressed [3];
- appropriate tests must be conducted at least yearly on all ICT systems and applications supporting critical or important functions [3];
- the programme should include tests such as vulnerability assessments and scans, open source analyses, network security assessments, gap analyses, physical security reviews, questionnaires, source code reviews where feasible, scenario-based tests, compatibility testing, performance testing, end-to-end testing, and penetration testing [4];
- central securities depositories and central counterparties must perform vulnerability assessments before deploying or redeploying new or existing applications, infrastructure components, and supporting ICT services for critical or important functions [4];
- other than microenterprises, certain financial entities must carry out advanced testing by means of TLPT at least every 3 years, with possible adjustment by the competent authority based on risk profile and circumstances [6];
- each TLPT must cover several or all critical or important functions, be performed on live production systems, and the scope must be validated by the competent authority [6].

The documents do not specify every operational detail of how each test must be run, but they do set out the required programme, minimum frequency for some tests, independence, remediation, and the main types of testing [1][3][4][6].

Under DORA Article 30, financial entities must include contractual provisions that clearly allocate the rights and obligations of the financial entity and the ICT third-party service provider in writing, and the full contract must include the service level agreements in one written document available in a paper or durable downloadable, accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the contract must cover:
- a clear and complete description of the functions and ICT services to be provided, including whether subcontracting of critical or important functions, or material parts of them, is permitted and under what conditions [1];
- the locations where the services and any subcontracted functions are provided and where data is processed or stored, plus advance notification of any planned location changes [1];
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality for data protection, including personal data [1];
- access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in an easily accessible format if the provider becomes insolvent, is resolved, discontinues business, or the contract is terminated [2];
- service level descriptions, including updates and revisions [2];
- assistance from the provider to the financial entity at no additional cost, or at a cost determined ex ante, when an ICT incident occurs [2];
- cooperation with competent authorities and resolution authorities [2];
- termination rights and minimum notice periods [2];
- participation in ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2][3].

For contracts supporting critical or important functions, additional provisions are required, including:
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets so the financial entity can monitor services and take corrective action without undue delay when service levels are not met [3];
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that may materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the services [3];
- business contingency plans and ICT security measures, tools and policies appropriate to the financial entity’s regulatory framework [3];
- details on the scope, procedures, and frequency of inspections and audits [4];
- exit strategies, including a mandatory adequate transition period to avoid disruption and to allow migration to another provider or in-house solutions [4].

The documents also say that, when negotiating these arrangements, financial entities and providers shall consider using standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities for specific services [4].

The documents do not specify every possible contractual clause beyond these listed elements, and they do not describe any penalty amounts or model wording for the clauses [1]-[4].
